<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Release overlay for side-loaded builds.

  Cleartext HTTP, for the same reason the debug overlay has it: a self-hosted
  Navidrome on a LAN is normally plain HTTP on a port, and without this a
  published APK simply cannot reach one. HTTPS servers are unaffected.

  This is a variant overlay, not src/main — the plugin validates src/main only,
  so a build submitted to Light never carries either of these. If a store build
  ever needs to talk to a LAN server, that is a conversation with Light about a
  network security config rather than something to smuggle in here.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      TEMPORARY SPIKE — see sdk/client/.../display/LightDisplayColor.kt.

      Lets the app switch LightOS's device-wide monochrome filter off while it is
      in front, which is the only route to colour album art on this phone: the
      filter is applied by SurfaceFlinger after composition, so nothing an app
      draws can defeat it.

      Here as well as in the debug overlay because the Settings screen carries a
      Color toggle. Shipping that toggle in a build where it *cannot* work is the
      misleading kind of control this project refuses to ship — so either the
      permission travels with it or the toggle goes.

      It does nothing on its own. The permission is signature|privileged and only
      grantable because of its `development` protection flag, so it stays inert
      until the user runs, once, on a machine with adb:

        adb shell pm grant com.sublunar.amp android.permission.WRITE_SECURE_SETTINGS

      Verified: without the grant the app launches, the filter stays on, and
      nothing throws — a clean no-op. The grant survives `install -r` and is lost
      on a full uninstall.

      Remove along with the spike when Light offers a sanctioned way to exempt a
      tool from the filter; see docs/SDK-GAPS.md §4.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.WRITE_SECURE_SETTINGS"
        tools:ignore="ProtectedPermissions" />

    <application android:usesCleartextTraffic="true" />
</manifest>
